    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\) and find the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy it, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Simplify the right-hand side of the equation by performing polynomial long division or synthetic division.\n2. Set the simplified right-hand side equal to 5.\n3. Solve the resulting equation for \\(x\\).\n4. Sum the \\(x\\)-values that are solutions to the equation.\n\nLet's start with the right-hand side of the equation:\n\n\\[5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\]\n\nWe can perform polynomial long division to simplify \\(\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\).\n\n1. Divide \\(x^3\\) by \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\).\n2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x+2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\).\n3. Subtract \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\) from \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n4. Divide \\(-4x^2\\) by \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\).\n5. Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x+2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n6. Subtract \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) to get 0.\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} = x^2 - 4x\\]\n\nNow, we set this equal to 5:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x = 5\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation:\n\n\\[(x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0\\]\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\(x = 5\\) and \\(x = -1\\). To find the sum of the \\(x\\)-values, we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
