      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5=\\frac{x^3-2x^2-8x}{x+2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. Notice that the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored by factoring out \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8).\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[x^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2).\\]\n\nThus, the numerator becomes:\n\n\\[x(x - 4)(x + 2).\\]\n\nSo the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}\\) simplifies to:\n\n\\[5 = x(x - 4) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x \\neq -2.\\]\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\n\\[5 = x(x - 4).\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can rewrite as:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\n\nWe factor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0.\\]\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation are:\n\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\\]\n\nWe need to check that these solutions do not make the original denominator zero. The denominator is \\(x + 2\\), and neither \\(x = 5\\) nor \\(x = -1\\) makes the denominator zero. Therefore, both solutions are valid.\n\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{4}.\\]",
