      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. Notice that the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored. We look for common factors:\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) = x(x - 4)(x + 2).\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[5 = x(x - 4).\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = -5\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 20}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm 6}{2}.\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{4 + 6}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 6}{2} = -1.\\]\n\nThus, the solutions to the equation are \\(x = 5\\) and \\(x = -1\\). The sum of these \\(x\\)-values is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
